.TH PATH_NUM_SA 3 "15 Mar 2023" "CB" "Library Functions"

.SH NAME
path_num_sa \- Copy the path up to a specified component to a stralloc

.SH SYNOPSIS
.IB #include <lib/path_internal.h>

.IB size_t
.IB path_num_sa(const char* p, size_t len, stralloc* sa, int n);

.SH DESCRIPTION
The \fBpath_num_sa\fR function copies the part of the path up to the specified component to a \fBstralloc\fR structure. It skips the first \fIn\fR components of the path and stores the remaining part in the \fBstralloc\fR.

.SH ARGUMENTS
.TP
.IB p
The input path string.
.TP
.IB len
The length of the input path string.
.TP
.IB sa
The \fBstralloc\fR structure where the path part will be copied.
.TP
.IB n
The number of components to skip.

.SH RETURN VALUE
The function returns the length of the path part that was copied to the \fBstralloc\fR.

.SH EXAMPLES
The following example demonstrates how to use the \fBpath_num_sa\fR function:

.nf
#include <stdio.h>
#include <lib/path_internal.h>
#include <lib/stralloc.h>

int main() {
    const char* path = "/usr/lib/bin";
    size_t len = strlen(path);
    stralloc sa;
    stralloc_init(&sa);
    size_t num_len = path_num_sa(path, len, &sa, 2);
    printf("Path length up to component 2: %zu\n", num_len);
    printf("Path part: %s\n", sa.s);
    stralloc_free(&sa);
    return 0;
}
.nef